l things so now he circles back and  he goes back and buys tops the same  company that was valued at 1.3 billion  lost the license didn't do their ipo or  didn't go public via spec and now he  buys them for 500 million dollars insane  insane insane business move very  strategic considering he literally  created if you really think about it  this business didn't exist six years ago  they've never sold a product never made  a product to my knowledge right they got  these licenses and they created 10  billion dollars of economic value out of  thin air fnatic still owns about 80  percent of it so they they created about  8 billion worth of  value for themselves and the other  shareholders  own about 20 of it so about two billion  dollars including investors and leagues  and so on so crazy crazy crazy business  move here extremely strategic extremely  smart and it's gonna be interesting to  see like how this goes right because if  you have a ten billion dollar company  now that went and acquired another  company has all these rights and stuff  like how big can this get obviously  investors believe in the future of this  and believe that it can get much bigger  but ultimately like this is just a  savage move  john what did you think when you saw it  michael rubin is one of  the best entrepreneurs i have seen in a  long time  i would put him up there with some of  the great entrepreneurs simply because  he's been an entrepreneur his entire  life starting with you know he did  wholesale for skiing equipment and  snowboarding equipment and he's moved  fanatics into i think a unique position  with these ip of the leagues i think  it's one of the most valuable things you  can get  in that space he's also he sold fanatics  re-bought fanatics back  and then also look he just got  distribution he just got manufacturing  and he just got a whole network of uh  resources along with that purchase of  tops it's a no-brainer for him and i  think the biggest flex possible is that  he's 